    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "---\n\nTo determine the maximum number of planes that can be formed by 12 points in space where no three points are collinear, we use combinatorial reasoning. Specifically, we need to find the number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12, because any set of 3 points determines a unique plane.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 points out of 12 is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{12}{3}\\). This is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(9!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{3! \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(3!\\):\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 11 = 132\n\\]\n\\[\n132 \\times 10 = 1320\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of planes that can be determined by 12 points in space is \\(\\boxed{220}\\).",
